1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,487
This is CanLab, a workstation for reverse engineering a CAN
bus.

2
00:00:04,487 --> 00:00:06,590
We start by loading a capture.

3
00:00:06,590 --> 00:00:13,040
This one is ten seconds of traffic in SavvyCAN format, about
six and a half thousand frames.

4
00:00:13,040 --> 00:00:15,213
The FRAMES tab is the raw view:

5
00:00:15,213 --> 00:00:22,014
every frame in time order, with its timestamp, arbitration ID,
bus, length, the eight data bytes,

6
00:00:22,014 --> 00:00:25,519
and the gap since the last frame with the same ID.

7
00:00:25,519 --> 00:00:31,899
A byte lights up green when it changed, so you can see at a
glance which parts of a message

8
00:00:31,899 --> 00:00:33,792
carry something that moves.

9
00:00:33,792 --> 00:00:38,518
On the left, every arbitration ID the capture contains,
grouped by bus,

10
00:00:38,518 --> 00:00:42,246
with how often it appears and how many frames there are.

11
00:00:42,246 --> 00:00:45,241
Ten IDs here, from one hertz up to a hundred.

12
00:00:45,241 --> 00:00:48,902
Selecting an ID opens it in the inspector on the right:

13
00:00:48,902 --> 00:00:54,760
the last ten frames in hex, a per-byte activity bar showing
which bytes actually change,

14
00:00:54,760 --> 00:00:57,689
and minimum, maximum and mean for each byte.

15
00:00:57,689 --> 00:01:02,016
That is usually the first question you ask of an unknown
message.

16
00:01:02,016 --> 00:01:05,377
The SIGNALS tab classifies every message for you.

17
00:01:05,377 --> 00:01:09,836
It runs offline, with no API key and nothing leaving the
machine.

18
00:01:09,836 --> 00:01:15,118
For each ID it reports the frame count, the rate, the entropy
of the payload,

19
00:01:15,118 --> 00:01:16,558
and a suspected role:

20
00:01:16,558 --> 00:01:22,732
a sensor carrying a physical value, a status message, a
counter, or a diagnostic response.

21
00:01:22,732 --> 00:01:27,534
These are heuristics that point you at the interesting
messages first,

22
00:01:27,534 --> 00:01:30,072
not identifications to trust blindly.

23
00:01:30,072 --> 00:01:31,895
AUTO-RE does the tedious part.

24
00:01:31,895 --> 00:01:35,662
It sweeps every message at once, looking for rolling counters,

25
00:01:35,662 --> 00:01:39,611
bytes that increment each frame and wrap, and for checksum
bytes,

26
00:01:39,611 --> 00:01:44,958
by testing whether a byte is the sum, the exclusive or, or the
nibble sum of the others.

27
00:01:44,958 --> 00:01:49,636
A match only counts if it beats simply guessing the byte's
most common value,

28
00:01:49,636 --> 00:01:55,287
so padding does not read as a checksum, and a relation that
holds for every byte of a message

29
00:01:55,287 --> 00:01:59,297
is thrown away rather than reported, because it localises
nothing.

30
00:01:59,297 --> 00:02:04,948
Counters and checksums are not signals, and knowing which
bytes they occupy removes them from

31
00:02:04,948 --> 00:02:05,616
the search.

32
00:02:05,616 --> 00:02:07,769
That sweep tells you which byte.

33
00:02:07,769 --> 00:02:14,093
To find out which algorithm, the CHECKSUM GUESSER takes one
message and one byte and tries all

34
00:02:14,093 --> 00:02:14,564
twelve:

35
00:02:14,564 --> 00:02:21,091
plain and inverted sums, two's complement, nibble sums, CRC-8
in its SAE J1850 and AUTOSAR forms,

36
00:02:21,091 --> 00:02:25,935
and the manufacturer variants used by Hyundai, Toyota, Honda
and Subaru.

37
00:02:25,935 --> 00:02:31,250
It fits on the first seventy per cent of the capture and
validates on the rest,

38
00:02:31,250 --> 00:02:37,575
and reports both numbers, so an algorithm that merely
memorised the training frames is visible

39
00:02:37,575 --> 00:02:41,208
as a high training score with a poor validation score.

40
00:02:41,208 --> 00:02:47,139
The same tab measures per-bit entropy across a message to
suggest where one signal ends and the

41
00:02:47,139 --> 00:02:47,889
next begins.

42
00:02:47,889 --> 00:02:50,074
Bits that never change are padding;

43
00:02:50,074 --> 00:02:53,508
bits that change constantly belong together in a value.

44
00:02:53,508 --> 00:02:58,128
It is a suggestion for the boundaries, which you then confirm
by decoding.

45
00:02:58,128 --> 00:02:59,668
Now we define a signal.

46
00:02:59,668 --> 00:03:03,285
The DBC BUILDER is where a guess becomes a definition.

47
00:03:03,285 --> 00:03:07,304
We are describing the front-left wheel speed on message 0A6:

48
00:03:07,304 --> 00:03:10,519
sixteen bits, big-endian, starting at bit seven,

49
00:03:10,519 --> 00:03:14,001
scaled by one thirty-second of a kilometre per hour.

50
00:03:14,001 --> 00:03:19,561
The bit grid underneath shows the live payload with the
selected field highlighted,

51
00:03:19,561 --> 00:03:23,780
and it converts between the grid and DBC bit numbering for
you,

52
00:03:23,780 --> 00:03:26,928
which is the part everybody gets wrong by hand.

53
00:03:26,928 --> 00:03:29,982
Underneath the editor is the live decode preview:

54
00:03:29,982 --> 00:03:35,902
real frames from the capture, their raw bytes, and the
physical value your definition produces.

55
00:03:35,902 --> 00:03:42,009
Sixty to eighty kilometres per hour here, which is the right
order of magnitude for a wheel speed,

56
00:03:42,009 --> 00:03:43,629
so the scale is plausible.

57
00:03:43,629 --> 00:03:49,736
Every decode in CanLab runs through cantools, the same library
that will read the file you export,

58
00:03:49,736 --> 00:03:52,416
so what you see here is what you get later.

59
00:03:52,416 --> 00:03:54,398
The PLOT tab draws it over time.

60
00:03:54,398 --> 00:03:59,105
Raw bytes and decoded signals can be plotted together on a
shared time axis,

61
00:03:59,105 --> 00:04:04,741
each with its own scale, so you can compare a decoded value
against the bytes it came from,

62
00:04:04,741 --> 00:04:07,219
or against a different message entirely.

63
00:04:07,219 --> 00:04:12,359
A wheel speed that rises and falls smoothly is a good sign the
definition is right;

64
00:04:12,359 --> 00:04:15,456
a sawtooth would tell you the byte order is wrong.

65
00:04:15,456 --> 00:04:19,412
INTELLIGENCE looks across messages rather than within one.

66
00:04:19,412 --> 00:04:25,756
It measures how often each ID repeats, so you can tell a
hundred-hertz control message from a

67
00:04:25,756 --> 00:04:27,393
one-hertz status report.

68
00:04:27,393 --> 00:04:31,417
It correlates bytes between different IDs with a lag sweep,

69
00:04:31,417 --> 00:04:36,192
which is how you find the same physical quantity reported by
two ECUs.

70
00:04:36,192 --> 00:04:40,899
It diffs two captures to show what changed when you pressed a
button.

71
00:04:40,899 --> 00:04:45,264
And it decodes J1939 parameter group numbers for heavy
vehicles.

72
00:04:45,264 --> 00:04:47,239
ML INTEL goes further per byte.

73
00:04:47,239 --> 00:04:53,228
It classifies each byte as a counter, a checksum, a boolean
flag, a physical value or padding,

74
00:04:53,228 --> 00:04:56,031
with a confidence and the entropy behind it.

75
00:04:56,031 --> 00:05:00,873
It fits a baseline from normal traffic and then scores frames
for anomalies,

76
00:05:00,873 --> 00:05:06,416
which is how you find the one message that behaves differently
when a fault is present.

77
00:05:06,416 --> 00:05:11,832
It also detects change points and finds messages with similar
behaviour by embedding.

78
00:05:11,832 --> 00:05:13,648
The DASHBOARD is the overview.

79
00:05:13,648 --> 00:05:18,371
The heatmap shows how much each byte of each message moves
across the capture,

80
00:05:18,371 --> 00:05:23,094
so a dense column is a message worth investigating and a blank
one is padding.

81
00:05:23,094 --> 00:05:26,666
There is a message timeline showing when each ID is active,

82
00:05:26,666 --> 00:05:30,057
and gauges you can point at any signal you have defined,

83
00:05:30,057 --> 00:05:34,416
so a decoded value can be watched as an instrument rather than
a number.

84
00:05:34,416 --> 00:05:36,673
TIMELINE is for questions about when.

85
00:05:36,673 --> 00:05:41,187
Several signals are stacked on one scrubbable axis with a
shared playhead,

86
00:05:41,187 --> 00:05:46,311
so you can line up the moment a flag flips against the moment
a value starts moving.

87
00:05:46,311 --> 00:05:51,252
It also syncs to a video of the dashboard or the road, with an
adjustable offset,

88
00:05:51,252 --> 00:05:54,912
so you can match what the bus did to what the car was doing.

89
00:05:54,912 --> 00:05:59,566
Once the definitions are right, CODE GEN turns them into a
working program.

90
00:05:59,566 --> 00:06:02,669
Pick the signals, the interface and the direction,

91
00:06:02,669 --> 00:06:08,440
and it writes Python that opens the bus and decodes those
signals, or encodes and sends them.

92
00:06:08,440 --> 00:06:14,273
The generated encoder goes through cantools and applies the
counter and checksum convention of

93
00:06:14,273 --> 00:06:19,920
whichever vehicle profile you have selected, so the frames it
produces are properly formed.

94
00:06:19,920 --> 00:06:24,120
Your work leaves in whatever format the next tool needs.

95
00:06:24,120 --> 00:06:27,946
A standard DBC that cantools and Vector tools read.

96
00:06:27,946 --> 00:06:33,346
An openpilot DBC with the comma dot ai checksum and counter
annotations.

97
00:06:33,346 --> 00:06:38,372
Vector CANdb++ with the cycle times measured from your own
capture.

98
00:06:38,372 --> 00:06:40,697
AUTOSAR ARXML four point three.

99
00:06:40,697 --> 00:06:46,847
And a Wireshark Lua dissector, so your signals appear by name
in a packet capture.

100
00:06:46,847 --> 00:06:53,823
Each of these is verified in the test suite by loading it back
with the tool that has to read

101
00:06:53,823 --> 00:06:54,048
it.

102
00:06:54,048 --> 00:06:56,782
DIAGNOSTICS is the request side.

103
00:06:56,782 --> 00:06:58,063
UDS, ISO 14229:

104
00:06:58,063 --> 00:07:06,264
read diagnostic trouble codes, read ECU identification, and
scan which services an ECU supports.

105
00:07:06,264 --> 00:07:13,440
That scan is read-only by default, because probing a service
like ECU Reset or Clear

106
00:07:13,440 --> 00:07:18,395
Diagnostics on a live bus does exactly what the name says.

107
00:07:18,395 --> 00:07:25,485
There is OBD-II mode one with automatic PID discovery, J1939,
a bus health monitor,

108
00:07:25,485 --> 00:07:30,696
XCP for reading ECU memory, and DoIP for diagnostics over IP.

109
00:07:30,696 --> 00:07:36,841
Security access, service 0x27, is the seed and key exchange
that unlocks a protected session.

110
00:07:36,841 --> 00:07:43,118
CanLab requests a seed and tries the common algorithms, or
your own key function from a script.

111
00:07:43,118 --> 00:07:46,686
Brute force is available for short keys, rate limited,

112
00:07:46,686 --> 00:07:51,575
and it stops the moment the ECU reports that the attempt limit
is reached,

113
00:07:51,575 --> 00:07:57,456
because tripping that counter can lock a module until it is
power cycled, or permanently.

114
00:07:57,456 --> 00:07:59,649
Everything so far only listened.

115
00:07:59,649 --> 00:08:02,869
Before anything can be sent, there is one gate.

116
00:08:02,869 --> 00:08:07,596
ARM TX is off by default and nothing leaves the tool while it
is off:

117
00:08:07,596 --> 00:08:11,913
not injection, not replay, not fuzzing, not gateway
forwarding,

118
00:08:11,913 --> 00:08:14,311
and not diagnostic requests either.

119
00:08:14,311 --> 00:08:15,887
The status bar says so.

120
00:08:15,887 --> 00:08:20,272
Turning it on requires confirming a warning that names the
risk,

121
00:08:20,272 --> 00:08:24,520
and turning it off again does not merely block the next frame,

122
00:08:24,520 --> 00:08:27,192
it stops anything already transmitting.

123
00:08:27,192 --> 00:08:32,623
With transmit armed, and only on an isolated bench, the
INJECTION tab can send.

124
00:08:32,623 --> 00:08:36,061
Set a signal to a physical value and send it once,

125
00:08:36,061 --> 00:08:41,079
or loop it at a chosen period with the counter and checksum
kept correct.

126
00:08:41,079 --> 00:08:46,304
Replay a capture back onto the bus at real speed or faster,
with a scrubber.

127
00:08:46,304 --> 00:08:49,811
Define trigger rules that fire on a byte condition.

128
00:08:49,811 --> 00:08:55,723
Sweep an actuator between limits with a watchdog that aborts
if a message goes silent.

129
00:08:55,723 --> 00:09:00,192
And run scripted test sequences of inject, wait and assert
steps.

130
00:09:00,192 --> 00:09:04,488
The GATEWAY tab bridges two CAN channels and puts you in
between.

131
00:09:04,488 --> 00:09:06,272
Rules are applied in order:

132
00:09:06,272 --> 00:09:12,022
pass a message through, block it, or modify a byte or the
arbitration ID as it crosses.

133
00:09:12,022 --> 00:09:17,177
That is how you test what an ECU does when a message it
depends on disappears,

134
00:09:17,177 --> 00:09:19,424
or arrives with a different value.

135
00:09:19,424 --> 00:09:25,438
It needs two hardware channels, and like everything else it
will not forward a single frame

136
00:09:25,438 --> 00:09:27,024
until transmit is armed.

137
00:09:27,024 --> 00:09:31,094
The OBD-II tab is the standardised subset any car will answer.

138
00:09:31,094 --> 00:09:34,049
It discovers which PIDs the vehicle supports,

139
00:09:34,049 --> 00:09:38,973
walking the continuation windows rather than assuming the
first thirty-two,

140
00:09:38,973 --> 00:09:43,109
and then polls the ones you pick and shows them as live
gauges:

141
00:09:43,109 --> 00:09:47,376
engine speed, coolant temperature, road speed, throttle
position.

142
00:09:47,376 --> 00:09:51,205
The AI ENGINE is optional and off unless you configure it.

143
00:09:51,205 --> 00:09:55,101
It sends one message ID's statistics to Anthropic, to Groq,

144
00:09:55,101 --> 00:10:01,043
or to a local Ollama model that never leaves your machine, and
asks for an interpretation.

145
00:10:01,043 --> 00:10:05,863
What makes it useful is that the offline analysis goes with
the question:

146
00:10:05,863 --> 00:10:09,692
the byte roles, the detected checksum, the message period.

147
00:10:09,692 --> 00:10:13,587
The model reasons about measured facts rather than raw hex.

148
00:10:13,587 --> 00:10:19,859
Its answers are still suggestions, and accepting one into the
DBC is refused unless it actually

149
00:10:19,859 --> 00:10:21,312
states a bit position.

150
00:10:21,312 --> 00:10:24,221
Connected to a real bus, frames stream in live.

151
00:10:24,221 --> 00:10:29,235
One receive thread reads the adapter and hands frames to
everyone who needs them,

152
00:10:29,235 --> 00:10:34,064
so a diagnostic scan and the live table never steal responses
from each other.

153
00:10:34,064 --> 00:10:37,840
Frames go into a capped buffer that appends in constant time,

154
00:10:37,840 --> 00:10:41,492
so a busy bus does not slow the tool down as the log grows:

155
00:10:41,492 --> 00:10:46,320
the frame counter and the rate keep climbing while the table
stays responsive.

156
00:10:46,320 --> 00:10:47,418
That is the loop:

157
00:10:47,418 --> 00:10:52,326
load a capture, let the offline analysis narrow it down,
define the signals,

158
00:10:52,326 --> 00:10:56,912
verify them by decoding real frames, and export to whatever
comes next.

159
00:10:56,912 --> 00:11:02,079
A project file saves the frames, the signal definitions and
your notes together.

160
00:11:02,079 --> 00:11:07,181
A REST API and an MCP server expose the same analysis to
scripts and to agents.

161
00:11:07,181 --> 00:11:10,992
And the one rule that does not change is the transmit gate:

162
00:11:10,992 --> 00:11:16,998
it is off until you turn it on, and you should only turn it on
when the bus you are connected

163
00:11:16,998 --> 00:11:18,096
to is on a bench.
